<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Convention People's Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as CPP</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Convention People's Party is a Ghanaian political party founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah">Kwame Nkrumah</a> in June 1949 after he broke with the United <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> Convention. Demanding 'self-government now', it won the elections of 1951, 1954 and 1956, led the Gold Coast to independence as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> in 1957 and became the only legal party in 1964, until the coup of 1966 overthrew Nkrumah.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/convention-peoples-party.jpg" alt="Convention People's Party" width="295" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Convention People's Party (CPP) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CPP_%E2%80%92_Convention_People%27s_Party_logo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The United Gold Coast Convention, formed in August 1947 and led mainly by professional and business men, invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah">Kwame Nkrumah</a> home from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> as its general secretary on the proposal of the lawyer Ebenezer Ako Adjei. Nkrumah had helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-padmore">George Padmore</a> organise the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifth-pan-african-congress">Fifth Pan-African Congress</a> in Manchester in 1945. He arrived late in 1947 amid discontent over shortages and rising prices, the uncompensated cutting-out of cocoa trees with swollen-shoot disease and the meagre gratuities of ex-servicemen, which led to a boycott of mostly foreign-owned firms from 26 January 1948.</p><p>In June 1949, after a three-day meeting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarkwa">Tarkwa</a>, the CYO agreed to form a new party that kept the name Convention. On 11 June the UGCC's Working Committee declared membership of the two bodies incompatible and announced charges against Nkrumah, and on 12 June, before a crowd of some 60,000 on the Old Polo Ground, he resigned and launched the CPP.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>While Nkrumah was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-fort">James Fort</a> prison, K. A. Gbedemah kept the party running, and its manifesto was smuggled out on toilet paper. The CPP swept the municipal elections of 1950 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-coast">Cape Coast</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a>. Its government moved the Motion of Destiny on 10 July 1953 asking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to name a date for independence, and in the first direct elections, on 19 June 1954, it won 72 of 104 seats. Against the National Liberation Movement, which called for a federal constitution, it won again in 1956, and at midnight on the night of 5 to 6 March 1957 Nkrumah proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>'s independence on the Polo Ground in Accra.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>On 24 February 1966, while Nkrumah was travelling on a Commonwealth mission seeking a resolution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> crisis, a military and police junta overthrew the CPP government. The party stayed banned, and its followers regrouped in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-national-party">People's National Party</a>, whose candidate Hilla Limann won the 1979 elections before Jerry John Rawlings overthrew him on 31 December 1981. After party politics returned in 1992 the Nkrumaist movement split into several parties. The CPP won three of 230 seats in 2004 and one in 2008, for Nkrumah's daughter Samia Nkrumah, and it joined the Socialist International as a consultative member in June 2018.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Convention People's Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kwame Nkrumah, who had attended the Fifth Pan-African Congress in 1945, returned in 1947 and in 1949 founded the <b>Convention People's Party</b> on a mass following of workers, farmers, ex-servicemen, teachers and market women.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast Colony · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
